# 🌀 Well, Well, Well

That is your theme. Build one complete game, of any genre, around it. The concept,
mechanics, look, and feel are yours to invent, with no specification and no reference
design to match.

The name is a pun. A **gravity well** is the dip a mass makes in space — the pull
that draws everything toward it — and "well, well, well" is the sound of a curious
discovery. So the name is a wink, not a spec: there is no required number of wells,
no fixed setting to reproduce. What the theme fixes is a feel and a force, and your
game must be built genuinely around **both**:

- **Gravity is the whole game.** Gravity is the dominant force of your game, not a bit
  of flavour on top of it. It shapes how things move, what the player can do, and how
  it feels to do it. If you removed gravity and the game still played the same, you
  have not built to this theme.
- **A star system that won't hold still.** Set the game in a place that pulls: worlds
  with real mass, in motion, tugging on everything — on each other, and on whatever
  the player controls. Straight lines don't exist here; every path bends around the
  wells it passes, and where the worlds are *now* is not where they'll be in a moment.

What the player actually *does* in a system like that — launch, fall, orbit, race,
thread, rescue, mine, dodge, or something you haven't thought of yet — is yours to
invent. Fusing "gravity rules everything" with "a live, moving system" into one
coherent, enjoyable game is the whole creative problem. Pick one strong idea and make
it genuinely good, rather than settling for the first thing that comes to mind.

Your project root is `{{workspace}}`. Build the game there.

## Your time budget

You have **about {{time_limit_hours}} hours** of wall-clock time for this run, after
which the session is stopped — whatever is committed at that point is what gets
published. Budget it: leave yourself room to build the assets, verify the game, and
commit before the clock runs out. You can check the current time at any point by
running `date` in the container, so you can see how much of the budget remains and
pace the work accordingly. Scope to finish comfortably rather than racing the
deadline.

## Your starting point

A starter project is already in place at `{{workspace}}`: a `package.json` that
pins **Playwright** as a dev dependency, with its dependencies already installed
(`node_modules/` is populated and headless **Chromium** is downloaded), so you can
